        {
          "name": "Redstone.Blocks.Group",
          "docstring": "    Group\n\nAn [assembly](@ref g-assembly) built on the fly, whose values are the ad-hoc\ntopologies.\n\nA `Group` is an assembly that holds its children and its declarations as field\nvalues. The elements of its `children` field, a `NamedTuple` of\n[components](@ref g-component), are its children. Its [`local_wires`](@ref),\n[`input_wires`](@ref), [`output_wires`](@ref) and [`sample_times`](@ref) are\nordinary functions of the instance, which return its fields.\n\nThe one declaration it adds is [`transparent_container`](@ref): `children` is\nname-transparent, so its elements go by bare key everywhere a child name\nappears. That is `\"ctl/out\" => \"plant/u\"` for a wire, `(ctl = Relative(2),)` for\na rate, `at(\"ctl\", …)` for a [condition](@ref g-condition) prefix and\n`\"plant/y\"` for a read path. A `Group`'s declarations are then textually\nidentical to a named assembly's. The rate declaration's field-name sugar, keying\non the field rather than on a path segment, keeps working as\n`(children = Relative(2),)` for the uniform case.\n\nThe type parameters carry the children's concrete types, so the\n[executor](@ref g-executor) specializes on them as on a named assembly. What a\n`Group` gives up against a named type is dispatch, which exploratory composition\ndoes not want.\n",
          "locations": [
            {
              "function": "sample_times",
              "file": "<depot>/packages/Redstone/sSbGy/src/blocks.jl",
              "line": 2123
            },
            {
              "function": "local_wires",
              "file": "<depot>/packages/Redstone/sSbGy/src/blocks.jl",
              "line": 2120
            },
            {
              "function": "input_wires",
              "file": "<depot>/packages/Redstone/sSbGy/src/blocks.jl",
              "line": 2121
            },
            {
              "function": "output_wires",
              "file": "<depot>/packages/Redstone/sSbGy/src/blocks.jl",
              "line": 2122
            },
            {
              "function": "transparent_container",
              "file": "<depot>/packages/Redstone/sSbGy/src/blocks.jl",
              "line": 2124
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "RedstoneExamples.Longitudinal",
          "docstring": "    Longitudinal(; m = 1000.0, S = 16.0, c̄ = 1.5, Iyy = 1800.0, T_max = 2500.0, ρ = 1.225,\n                   CL0 = 0.3, CLα = 5.0, CD0 = 0.03, k = 0.05,\n                   Cm0 = 0.05, Cmα = -1.0, Cmq = -12.0, Cmδe = -1.2)\n\nA rigid aircraft in its plane of symmetry, with three degrees of freedom:\nairspeed `V`, flight path angle `γ`, pitch angle `θ`, pitch rate `q` and\naltitude `h` as its states, elevator `δe` and throttle `δt` as its inputs, and\nthe states with the angle of attack `α = θ - γ` as its outputs, all from the\nstate, so the plant has no feedthrough. A continuous leaf. Lift and drag follow\na linear lift curve and a parabolic polar, the pitching moment a static, a\ndamping and an elevator term, and the thrust is `δt T_max` along the body\naxis, with constant density and still air:\n\n    V̇ = (T cos α - D) / m - g sin γ\n    γ̇ = (L + T sin α) / (m V) - g cos γ / V\n    θ̇ = q\n    q̇ = M / Iyy\n    ḣ = V sin γ\n\nThe defaults are a light single-engine aircraft. The parameters, the trim and\nthe modes are `longitudinal_aircraft.md`, sections 1 to 3.\n\nThe example is didactic, and it may change in a minor version.\n",
          "locations": [
